# Serving-node .env delta (milestone #4, #538) -- append to a copy of the
# central stack's .env, on the node, then correct every value marked REQUIRED.
#
# Why a delta and not a standalone file: the serving role reads the same
# application settings as the rest of the stack (cache dirs, retention, OCR
# defaults, the Inngest keys). Starting from the central .env keeps those
# identical and leaves only the handful of values that are genuinely per-node.
#
# Used with: docker compose -f docker-compose.serving-node.yml up -d

# --- REQUIRED: central Postgres, as this node reaches it -------------------
# The central stack's own value is `postgres`, a name that resolves only inside
# its compose network. Use the address this machine can reach -- normally the
# central host's private-network (e.g. WireGuard) IP.
#
# Note the DATABASE_URL line inherited from the central .env still says
# `@postgres:5432`. The compose file overrides it for the container, so it is
# harmless, but correct it anyway if you ever run a host-native process here.
DOCIE_DB_HOST=
DOCIE_DB_PORT=5432

# --- REQUIRED: how the central api reaches THIS node ------------------------
# Every deploy on this node records its endpoint as http://{this}:{port}/v1,
# and the central api resolves that string in ITS own network. A compose
# service name therefore resolves there to the CENTRAL node's container, and
# requests for this node's models land on the wrong machine, answered by
# whatever happens to hold the same port -- HTTP 200, wrong model (#538).
#
# Use this node's private-network IP, or a name that resolves fleet-wide to it.
# Never `serving`, never `localhost`.
DOCIE_SERVING_ADVERTISE_HOST=

# --- REQUIRED: which interface the runtime ports are published on -----------
# A deployed runtime carries no authentication of its own; the api gateway in
# front of it is what checks the key. Publishing the port window on a public
# interface hands anyone who can reach this host a free inference endpoint.
# Normally the same address as DOCIE_SERVING_ADVERTISE_HOST.
DOCIE_SERVING_PUBLISH_IP=

# --- REQUIRED: the central stack's POSTGRES_PASSWORD -----------------------
# Inherited from the copied .env. Keep it identical -- this node connects to
# the central database, it does not run one.
#POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# --- Node identity ---------------------------------------------------------
# Leave empty and the reconciler generates one on first publish and persists it
# in $DOCIE_SERVING_HOME/node_id, where it survives a restart on the same
# volume. Set a readable name if you prefer; it must be UNIQUE across the
# fleet, since it is what keeps this node's capacity row and placements
# distinguishable from every other node's (#529).
DOCIE_SERVING_NODE_ID=

# --- Inngest: the central server, as this node reaches it -------------------
# Already env-driven, but the central stack's values are compose service names.
# Replace the host in both; the ports are unchanged. Connect is OUTBOUND, so
# this node needs no inbound reachability for job delivery -- only these two
# endpoints and Postgres above.
INNGEST_BASE_URL=http://CENTRAL_HOST:8288
INNGEST_CONNECT_GATEWAY_URL=ws://CENTRAL_HOST:8289/v0/connect
# INNGEST_EVENT_KEY / INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY: inherited, keep identical.

# --- Capacity and ports ----------------------------------------------------
# Deploys allocate from this window, and the compose file publishes exactly the
# same range, so the two cannot disagree -- a runtime on an unpublished port
# would record an endpoint nothing can reach. Nodes do NOT need disjoint
# windows: the endpoint carries the host, so every node may use the same ports
# on its own address. Widen if this node should hold more concurrent runtimes
# (Docker runs one proxy per published port, so keep it to what you need).
DOCIE_SERVING_PORT_RANGE_START=8088
DOCIE_SERVING_PORT_RANGE_END=8107

# This node's RAM ceiling for the container, which is also the budget the
# sizing engine prices against for this node. Set it to what the machine
# actually has to spare.
DOCIE_SERVING_MEM_LIMIT=8g

# --- Image -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The build compiles llama.cpp and installs the encoder + embedding extras --
# a long build to repeat on every machine. Point this at a registry tag to pull
# instead; leave empty to build locally.
DOCIE_SERVING_IMAGE=

# Optional: host Ollama blobs, so seed-from-ollama can import an existing GGUF
# rather than re-downloading it here.
#OLLAMA_MODELS_HOST=/root/.ollama/models
